4. Emoji artwork (SIL Open Font License 1.1 and Apache-2.0 — both apply)
------------------------------------------------------------------------

    assets/neon/     — the 80 emoji images compiled into the firmware: our
                       restyled derivatives of Noto Color Emoji, generated by
                       tools/stylize_emoji.py (black-background compositing,
                       saturation boost, soft blur, additive halo — a mechanical
                       per-image filter). This is the only emoji input the build
                       consumes: tools/conv_emoji.py reads this directory and
                       converts it to RGB565 C arrays. What it actually converts
                       is the 80-codepoint EMOJI table hardcoded in that script;
                       the src/CMakeLists.txt glob over this directory only
                       triggers a rebuild. The two agree today (80 = 80), but
                       adding a PNG here does not put it in the firmware.
    assets/noto512/  — twelve unmodified Noto Color Emoji originals. NOT
                       compiled into the firmware; used only by
                       tools/gen_readme_icons.py to render the README icons.
    docs/emoji/      — the twelve README icons themselves: 96px LANCZOS
                       downscales of assets/noto512, produced by
                       tools/gen_readme_icons.py. These are Modified Versions,
                       as the neon set is, and likewise NOT compiled into the
                       firmware.

All three sets come from the Noto Emoji project, Copyright 2013 Google LLC —
https://github.com/googlefonts/noto-emoji, the 512x512 png/512/emoji_u*.png
bitmaps at commit 8998f5dd (2025-09-12). The noto512 files are byte-identical
to upstream, and every neon file was generated from the same-named upstream
bitmap (both verified 2026-08-10). stylize_emoji.py takes the source bitmaps
via --cat as a locally assembled catalog directory holding unmodified copies of
the png/512 files. Those copies sit in named subdirectories, and the names are
load-bearing rather than decorative: the script converts only the subdirectories
listed in --groups, whose default (emotion_face,heart,arrow,symbol) does not
reproduce this set. The neon images were produced from the catalog's `all`
directory (3731 files, the full png/512 set) with --style neon_feat, and the 80
shipped here were then picked by hand out of that output and copied into
assets/neon/ — the selection intent is recorded in tools/conv_emoji.py, not in
any script argument. All 80 filenames are present under `all` (verified
2026-08-10).

License: upstream says two different things about this artwork, and the
disagreement cannot be settled from upstream alone. This tree therefore does
not pick a winner — it carries both license texts and satisfies both sets of
obligations.

What upstream says, and where the contradiction came from (checked 2026-08-11):

  - The README assigns licenses per directory: the fonts subdirectory is under
    "SIL Open Font License, version 1.1"; "Tools and most image resources are
    under the Apache license, version 2.0"; third_party/region-flags is public
    domain. Read literally, the png/512 bitmaps used here are image resources,
    so Apache-2.0. That wording is deliberate: commit 51a7d28c6b (2016-10-20),
    "Clarify copyright status of image data in the main README", is what
    widened "Tools" to "Tools and most image resources".
  - Issue #45 ("License for pngs and svgs?") asked upstream this exact
    question. The maintainer answered the same day: "Apache for the source
    data. Flag images are public domain, or copyright-free, there's a list. I
    updated the README.md file."
  - But that README sentence links to ./LICENSE, which at the commit we use
    holds the OFL 1.1 text. It did not always: ./LICENSE carried the full
    Apache-2.0 text from 2015 until commit 254596e5f8 (2024-07-29, "Update
    LICENSE to OFL") replaced it. That commit touched LICENSE and nothing
    else — the README's Apache-2.0 sentence and its link were left as they
    were, which is how the two came to disagree.
  - png/ has no license file of its own, so reading by directory inheritance
    lands on OFL 1.1 through the root file.
  - The bitmaps are not extracted from the OFL font; they are the font's build
    input (Makefile: EMOJI_SRC_DIR ?= png/128, and the README: "The assets
    provided in the repo are all those used to build the NotoColorEmoji
    font"). So "OFL because it came out of a font" does not hold.
  - Apache-2.0 also appears at svg/LICENSE (Copyright 2013 Google, Inc.) and
    third_party/color_emoji/LICENSE, but only as the 13-line boilerplate
    header; upstream ships no full Apache-2.0 text anywhere.

On this evidence the Apache-2.0 reading is the stronger one, and redistributors
who review licensing read it that way: Debian's fonts-noto-color-emoji
copyright file, updated 2025-09-15 and so after the 2024 swap, still maps
fonts/* to OFL and everything else to Apache-2.0. Against that, the
repository's own machine-readable state at 8998f5dd says OFL 1.1, and no
upstream statement since the swap addresses png/ — the question has not been
put to upstream. So it is not settled, and picking a side would silently drop
the other one's obligations: Apache-2.0 requires its own terms to ship, which
including the OFL text does not accomplish, and vice versa. Both apply here:

  Attribution — Noto Emoji, Copyright 2013 Google LLC / Google, Inc.,
  https://github.com/googlefonts/noto-emoji at commit 8998f5dd.
  Texts — licenses/OFL-1.1.txt and licenses/Apache-2.0.txt.

licenses/OFL-1.1.txt is byte-identical to upstream's ./LICENSE at that commit
(sha256 500bb1cc…, checked 2026-08-10). licenses/Apache-2.0.txt is the canonical
Apache-2.0 text from apache.org (§1), so it carries no copyright holder's name
of its own and serves both the Waveshare code in §1 and the artwork here.

Points that matter for this firmware:

  - OFL §2 and Apache-2.0 §4(a) both want the license text to travel with the
    files. Both texts are in licenses/, and binary distributions must carry
    them (see ../README.txt).
  - Apache-2.0 §4(b) wants modified files to say so. A PNG cannot carry an
    in-file notice the way the vendor sources in §1 do, so the modification
    record above is the practical equivalent rather than literal compliance.
  - Upstream ships no NOTICE file, so Apache-2.0 §4(d) adds nothing to carry
    over here — same as §1.
  - No Reserved Font Name is declared (the copyright line carries no "with
    Reserved Font Name"), so OFL §3 imposes no renaming on derivatives.
  - Under OFL §5 the restyled neon derivatives stay OFL 1.1 on that reading.
  - For selling the devkit: shipping the images inside the firmware and selling
    the devkit is permitted on either reading — Apache-2.0 imposes no such
    restriction, and OFL §2 expressly allows bundling with software and selling
    that. Selling the emoji artwork on its own is permitted only under the
    Apache-2.0 reading, since OFL §1 forbids it. Do not do that until upstream
    has been asked and has answered.


